  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Using remote-sensing data from 2000 to 2020, this study evaluated the spatiotemporal interaction between built-up area growth and carbon stocks across China's Chengdu-Chongqing urban agglomeration. The authors combined the InVEST model with province-specific, stock-volume-derived forest biomass densities, finding that built-up land expanded from 3,140.3 km² to 15,550.5 km² over the twenty-year period. While regional carbon storage grew overall, the coupling coordination degree between expansion and storage showed divergent spatial trends, rising slightly from 0.368 to 0.394 at the city scale while declining from 0.557 to 0.490 at the county scale.",
      "why": "By incorporating local biomass expansion factors into carbon modeling, the study provides scale-sensitive empirical measurements demonstrating how core urban expansion undermines ecological coordination even when regional carbon totals increase."
    }
  },
